From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 20:55:19 -0400
Subject: feat: a pinned tab's x asks before removing it

GitHub issue 50 (pins H29; BACKLOG S-332): the close button on a Space pin
that was not on screen had put it to sleep, like the one on screen, and
removing a pin took the tab menu's Unpin and then a close. Now a Space pin
that is awake, on screen or not, shows a minus, which puts it to sleep at
its home and keeps it (H3); once it is asleep at its home it shows an x
that asks first: a bubble at the button, "Remove this pinned tab?" with
Remove and Cancel, Cancel the default, so Return alone removes nothing. The
button turns from one to the other as the pin sleeps or wakes, under the
pointer too. Remove closes the row, both panes of a split, as a row's close
button closes a tab, so the pin goes with it and Reopen Closed Tab can bring
it back. Cmd-W, a middle click and the tab menu keep H3's rule, and with
Cmd-W's setting off the button closes as before.

GitHub issue 63 (tabs R64, menus M1; BACKLOG S-363): the short tab menu
offers Chromium's Duplicate, above Copy Link. Its copy of an essential or
a Space pin, from the menu or the command Duplicate Tab, is a plain tab
added where a new tab opens, as Cmd-T adds one, and the essential or the
pin stays the only one. Chromium put the copy beside the tab it copies,
so an essential's copy was a second essential and a pin's copy landed
beside the pin. A plain tab's copy still lands right after it.

Why: the owner asked for it, with a confirmation so the user really
confirms a removal, and then for the minus first on every pin that is
awake, so a press only puts a pin to sleep and removing one takes a second
press and a confirmation. The owner chose a plain tab for a pin's copy
on 2026-10-30.
Removable when: never as a feature; the tab_view.cc and tab_close_button
hunks go if Chromium's vertical tab strip grows its own pinned-tab close
rule; the DuplicateTabAt hunk stays while essentials are Chromium's
pinned tabs.
